window.__BUNDLE__={"generatedAt":"2026-11-12T01:16:23.865668+00:00","recentDays":7,"archiveDays":1825,"recentCutoff":"2026-11-05","archiveCutoff":"2021-11-13","addedToday":0,"addedThisRun":0,"fetchStats":{"hf":238,"arxiv":1352,"total":1590},"freshness":{"latestPaperDate":"2026-11-11","latestPublishedDate":"2026-11-11","latestBatchCount":288,"newInLast24h":332,"newInLast48h":996},"historyTotal":32150,"topicCounts":{"Multi-Agent":3323,"Evaluation":17695,"Planning":2319,"Memory":626,"Code Agent":1291,"Interpretability":1076,"Agentic RAG":1624,"Agentic Search":2188,"RL for LLM":1653,"Reasoning":8466,"Hallucination":1350,"Post-Training":3496,"Alignment":4138,"World Model":638,"Tool Use":1509,"Agent Safety":2831,"Computer Use":409,"Reflection":1157,"VLM Training":2557,"LLM Training":4125,"Fine-tuning":4973,"Multimodal Agent":184,"Agent Framework":1002,"Error Attribution":195,"Data Engine":644},"count":36,"recentTotal":1853,"archiveCount":0,"archiveTotal":30297,"sources":{"hf":32,"arxiv":1821,"semanticScholar":0},"archiveSources":{"hf":0,"arxiv":0,"semanticScholar":0},"warnings":[],"notes":["archive is split by year under data/archive/ for fast lazy loading; data/history.json keeps the complete database"],"papers":[{"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":75,"title":"Tree-Ising: Repairing Language Models with Bit-Level Ising Edits","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14210","id":"arxiv:2611.14210","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14210","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14210.pdf","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.14210","authors":["Hadi Salloum","Ammar Ali"],"abstract":"Weight-space programming, introduced in recent MIT work by Takehi et al., enables a frontier LLM agent to read a network's weights and write a repair. Their GPT-6 Astra agent corrected the reasoning loops of Qwen3.5-0.8B without gradients, using 158 agent turns, 89.7 million tokens, and 50 forward-pass requests. Our method, Tree-Ising, casts this repair as a sparse discrete optimization problem over the model's own BF16 weights. Forward statistics define an objective that weakens a loop direction, strengthens a concluding direction, and preserves the layer's output on productive tokens. Within a state-tree representation, where lossless BF16 storage acts as a special case, the admissible edits are sign flips and exponent shifts that preserve all shared group exponents and mantissas. We decompose this objective exactly into independent QUBO (Ising) problems, one per output row, which we s"},{"tags":["Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":25,"title":"MMDM: Measuring Massive Multitask Decision Making","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14309","id":"arxiv:2611.14309","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14309","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14309.pdf","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.14309","authors":["Junhao Shen","Qing Zong","Yue Guo","Zhanqiu Zhang","Yiwen Guo","Hong Cheng"],"abstract":"System-1 Decision Models directly make structured probabilistic decisions over query-specified options given a state in a single forward pass, without autoregressively generating reasoning trajectories or textual responses. Recent evaluations cover diverse tasks but often rely on existing benchmarks, where prior exposure to test material can obscure generalization. Moreover, real-world decision making often involves long contexts, large option spaces, and complex logical dependencies, challenges that existing benchmarks do not adequately capture together. Thus, we introduce MMDM (Massive Multitask Decision Making), a benchmark of 5,448 high-quality questions across 16 task categories, combining newly constructed tasks with source-grounded recompositions and rewrites for single-select, multi-select, and soft-label decision making in fresh contexts. It covers realistic scenarios such as ag"},{"tags":["Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"upvotes":4,"title":"EarthRecord: From Pixels to Land Change with Multimodal Agents over Four Decades of Satellite Imagery","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12934","id":"arxiv:2611.12934","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12934","submittedDate":"2026-11-06","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12934.pdf","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.12934","authors":["Haoran Zhang","Huizhen Sun","Hanbin Wen","Jie Leng","Jiaxin Zhu","Zhaohua Sun"],"abstract":"While multimodal agents now carry out complex data analysis with tools, their ability to turn decades of satellite imagery into reliable land-change analysis, crucial for Earth observation science, remains unmeasured. Existing benchmarks fall short: they pose questions on curated images or give agents preprocessed inputs, leaving out the raw multi-sensor archives where such analysis begins. We introduce EarthRecord, a benchmark built on the raw record of 56 sites: four decades of Landsat, MODIS, VIIRS, and Sentinel-2 data, with cloud masking and cross-sensor harmonization left to the agent. It poses 238 land-change questions across ten analysis tasks, scored against withheld references from global products, agreement among methods, and visual interpretation. Two controls test what answers rest on: the closed book removes the record, and instrument pairs alter one satellite's data while n"},{"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":3,"title":"EVAThink: Evidence Sufficiency-Guided Training for Adaptive Thinking in Video Understanding","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13907","id":"arxiv:2611.13907","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13907","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13907.pdf","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.13907","authors":["Changti Wu","Ruizhe Fan","Bin Wang","Lei Zhang","Yuxiang Zhang"],"abstract":"Under a fixed frame budget, video understanding with vision-language models (VLMs) depends on whether the sampled frames provide sufficient evidence for the question. Sufficient frame evidence supports direct answering, whereas less informative observations may benefit from reasoning that connects the available cues. We introduce EVAThink, which enhances video understanding through evidence sufficiency-guided training for adaptive thinking. First, Recoverable Evidence Gap Mining constructs evidence-dense and evidence-sparse video views that share the same question and answer under an identical frame budget. It retains pairs where sparse views yield lower direct-answer accuracy than dense views but benefit from explicit reasoning, providing supervision for response-mode selection. Second, Evidence-Guided Two-Stage Reinforcement Learning uses stage-specific rewards to guide training with e"},{"tags":["Post-Training"],"upvotes":3,"title":"Are Dense Rewards Worth Their Tokens in GRPO?","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13560","id":"arxiv:2611.13560","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13560","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13560.pdf","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Post-Training"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.13560","authors":["Vikash Singh","Weicong Chen","Debargha Ganguly","Yanyan Zhang","Mohsen Hariri","Sreehari Sankar","Wang Yang","Chaoda Song","Alexander Nemecek","Osama Zafar","Yuting Shao","Kai Ye","Biyao Zhang","Xinpeng Li","Shouren Wang","Erman Ayday","Jing Ma","Sanmukh Rao Kuppannagari","Vipin Chaudhary"],"abstract":"RLVR trains language models to reason by rewarding correct final answers. A widely used algorithm, group-relative policy optimization (GRPO), gives every token of a solution the same credit, so dense process rewards have been proposed to credit intermediate steps, for example with a Monte-Carlo reward that scores each step by sampling continuations from it. Comparing such a reward with GRPO per training update, or vetting it with a gradient test on a single batch, leaves out what it costs: in our setting, the continuations alone take 91% of all generated tokens. We introduce a matched-compute protocol that gives every training intervention the same budget, measured in generated tokens and training GPU-hours, and tests it in preregistered studies with controls for side effects. Across 1,668 runs on six models, matching compute reverses the ranking. On Qwen2.5-1.5B, the Monte-Carlo reward "},{"tags":["Evaluation"],"upvotes":48,"title":"Don't Just Trust Your Memory: Evidence-Linked Memory Reduces Memory-Induced Degradation in LLM Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14320","id":"arxiv:2611.14320","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14320","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14320.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Evaluation"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.14320","authors":["Yukyeong Lee","Yumin Choi","Jinheon Baek","Sung Ju Hwang"],"abstract":"External memory enables LLM agents to improve from experience by distilling trajectories of past tasks into concise lessons for new tasks. However, memory can also lead to memory-induced degradation, where an agent fails on tasks it would solve without memory, often because it follows a lesson that is incorrect, or misapplies or misreads a valid one. This is because lessons are inferred from trajectories labeled only with task success or failure, yet are typically provided without the trajectories they came from, leaving the agent no way to check them. To address this, we introduce WikiTrace, an evidence-linked memory system designed to let agents check each lesson against its original trajectory. Specifically, motivated by the finding that a trajectory often still holds the evidence needed to catch an erroneous lesson, WikiTrace stores each lesson as a concise insight linked to a summar"},{"tags":["Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"upvotes":9,"title":"PhyRubric: Rubric-Grounded Diagnostic Evaluation of Physical Commonsense in Text-to-Video Generation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13576","id":"arxiv:2611.13576","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13576","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13576.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.13576","authors":["Huiyi Chen","Weitai Kang","Shicheng Fan","Xinshuo Wang","Zhixiang Shen","Yan Yan","Philip S. Yu","Baoxin Li"],"abstract":"As text-to-video (T2V) models are increasingly positioned as world simulators, it is essential to evaluate whether their videos conform to physical commonsense. However, existing benchmarks rely on annotators who rate each video with a single score based on intuition, yielding ratings that are subjective and do not tell which physical principle is violated. We introduce PhyRubric, a rubric-grounded diagnostic framework for evaluating physical commonsense in T2V generation. For each caption, PhyRubric constructs a caption-specific rubric before any video is seen, decomposing the caption into semantic and physical items, and derives scores from item-level verdicts grounded in timestamped evidence. We instantiate this framework as PhyRubric-Bench, which spans 34 categories across five physical domains, and scale it up to PhyRubric-Train with 3,788 videos, on which we fine-tune the open-sour"},{"tags":["Interpretability"],"upvotes":5,"title":"Are Causal Neuron Circuits Unique and Transferable?","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13885","id":"arxiv:2611.13885","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13885","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13885.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Interpretability"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.13885","authors":["Ömer Tekin Yavuz"],"abstract":"Interpretability demonstrations often show that intervening on a group of neurons changes a model's answer. A recent example removes an 18-neuron group in Qwen3-32B and the model answers \"Simba\" instead of \"Scar\" to a two-hop Lion King riddle. Such a demonstration establishes that the group matters, but not how much of it is necessary, whether other groups could do the same, or whether the effect carries over to other inputs. We ask: when intervening on a group of neurons changes a model's answer, how much of that group is actually necessary? For every selected group we measure all 4,096 subsets of 12 MLP neurons. On a two-alternative capital task (Qwen3-0.6B), zero-ablating 12 selected neurons lowers the answer margin by 27% while random neurons barely change it, yet replacing the same neurons with their values from other questions also barely changes it: their activations are nearly co"},{"tags":["Evaluation","Alignment"],"upvotes":1,"title":"Prompt2Echo (P2Echo): Text-Prompted Multi-View Echocardiography Segmentation with One Model","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13425","id":"arxiv:2611.13425","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13425","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13425.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Evaluation","Alignment"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.13425","authors":["Moein Heidari","Alvin Kimbowa","Mahdi Tabatabaei","Afshin Bozorgpour","Sina Ghorbani Kolahi","River Jiang","Wenjin Chen","David J. Foran","Jasmine Grewal","Dorit Merhof","Ilker Hacihaliloglu"],"abstract":"Automated segmentation in multi-view echocardiography is essential for comprehensive cardiac assessment, yet remains challenging due to substantial anatomical and appearance variability across standard acoustic windows. In this paper, we present P2Echo, a unified framework capable of segmenting diverse cardiac structures across multiple standard echocardiographic views using a single model controlled by structured view-aware text prompts. Unlike recent foundation model adaptations that rely on implicit latent priors, P2Echo explicitly conditions the segmentation process via a novel Dynamic Image-Text Alignment (DyITA) module. DyITA integrates a differentiable routing mechanism and token differential operators to dynamically modulate decoder features according to the requested view and target structure. This allows the network to adaptively switch its processing pathways for different vie"},{"tags":["Alignment"],"upvotes":1,"title":"Visual Similarity in Adult Word Extension and Artificial Word Learning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14116","id":"arxiv:2611.14116","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14116","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14116.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Alignment"],"hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2611.14116","authors":["Patricio M. Vera"],"abstract":"When two objects share a name, how much does their visual similarity explain, and what do the teaching examples add? First, we reanalyzed trial-level data from a large adult word-extension study (Lewis & Frank, 2018), computing each test image's distance to the examples shown in the space of a frozen self-supervised visual encoder (DINOv2). Distance was associated with extension (log odds -3.23 per standard deviation; 520 people), and an exploratory post hoc analysis suggested that this association persisted within the taught domain after controlling for taxonomic level and teaching condition. Example composition also mattered: after three examples, adults extended a word to 1.1% of superordinate items when the examples were subordinate and to 77.5% when they were superordinate. Second, in an artificial learner using the same encoder, words joining two categories were learned less accura"},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"upvotes":0,"title":"ChipOmni: A General-Purpose AI Agent for Electronic Design Automation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13020","id":"arxiv:2611.13020","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13020","submittedDate":"2026-11-09","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13020.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Pengkun Wang","Rui Mao","Zhe Zhao","Dongting Xie","Haibin Wen","Song Xu","Di Wu","Xu Wang","Zhengyang Zhou","Binwu Wang","Xiaofeng Cao","Yang Wang"],"abstract":"Electronic design automation (EDA) workflows remain highly fragmented, requiring engineers to coordinate heterogeneous representations, specialised software tools, and iterative verification across multiple design stages. Although large language models have brought more general reasoning and code-generation capabilities to EDA, many existing approaches remain focused on isolated tasks and struggle to plan, verify, and repair designs autonomously within executable engineering environments. Here we introduce ChipOmni, a knowledge-enhanced, context-evolving AI agent framework for EDA. ChipOmni integrates multimodal understanding, dynamic planning, multi-agent collaboration, and verification-driven tool interaction into a unified autonomous system. By continually incorporating feedback from external engineering tools, the framework maintains an evolving task context, integrates domain knowle"},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Evaluating LLM-Generated World Models for Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14419","id":"arxiv:2611.14419","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14419","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14419.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"authors":["Souhaila Djaffal","Amna Jamal","Gagana Madhugiri Devaraja","Anidipta Pal","Jobst Heitzig"],"abstract":"Formal world models can support planning, verification, and safety reasoning, but constructing them from open-ended natural-language situations is difficult, particularly in multi-agent settings where several formalizations may be plausible. We study whether five LLMs can generate such world models as partially observable stochastic games and whether mechanical validity is sufficient to establish that the resulting model faithfully represents the intended decision problem. We introduce a benchmark of 44 scenarios spanning game, situational, and cultural settings and evaluate generated world models along three dimensions: mechanical validity, reference-relative structural agreement, and semantic criteria. Across three empirical tracks, we analyze 410 first-pass generations. In the reference-backed game track, 134/135 generated world models pass a strict mechanical audit, yet mean permutat"},{"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Memory","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Coding Agent Memory Post-Training II: Unlocking the Memory Potential of Pre-trained File Operations for Long-Horizon Tasks via On-Policy Distillation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14009","id":"arxiv:2611.14009","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14009","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14009.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Memory","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"authors":["Lirui Luo","Kelong Mao","Xinwei Yang","Rongqing Li","Luyu Chen","Kieran Wong","Yudong Guo","Xinrui Wang","Jiayin Zhu","Gu Simiu","Sulong Xu","Cong Fang"],"abstract":"Language-model agents increasingly tackle long-horizon tasks whose interaction histories exceed the model's active context. They must therefore decide what to write down and what to read back, and when to do either. Recent work has begun to use on-policy distillation (OPD) to make memory control part of the policy, relying on memory tools and compaction protocols that lie outside either model's pre-training. However, this weakens the distillation on both sides: the teacher cannot give useful guidance on an interface it has never used, and the student samples too few meaningful memory actions for that guidance to take effect. To study this problem, we introduce Coding Agent Memory Gym II (CAMG-II), a suite of long-horizon agentic-RL environments spanning Shop, Coding, AutoResearch, General, and Visual. Alongside each environment's native task interface, CAMG-II provides ordinary Bash acce"},{"tags":["Agent Safety","VLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Textual Response-Aware Guidance for Zero-Shot Adversarial Robustness of Vision-Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40309","id":"arxiv:2610.40309","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40309","submittedDate":"2026-11-09","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40309.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Agent Safety","VLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Yubo Cui","Xianchao Guan","Zheng Zhang"],"abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) exhibit strong zero-shot generalization but remain vulnerable to adversarial attacks. Existing adversarial fine-tuning methods often use pre-trained model guidance to preserve image--text alignment while improving robustness. Our analysis of visual representation drift reveals complementary limitations of two representative forms of guidance. Matching visual features provides broad coverage by constraining all directions equally, but lacks semantic emphasis. Matching prediction distributions prioritizes semantically relevant directions, but remains limited by the candidate texts and prediction probabilities. These limitations can hinder preservation of pre-trained image--text relationships and degrade zero-shot performance. To address these issues, we propose Textual Response-Aware Guidance (TRAG), which constrains visual representation drift using the pre-t"},{"tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Seeing Is Not Believing: Counterfactual Lesion Erasure Teaches a Small Medical Vision-Language Agent to Report Only What the Image Shows","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13006","id":"arxiv:2611.13006","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13006","submittedDate":"2026-11-08","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13006.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Bhaskar Gurram"],"abstract":"Medical vision-language models (VLMs) often report a finding because the question names it; standard benchmarks miss this. From public segmentation datasets we build counterfactual views without manual annotation: the lesion erased by inpainting, a same-shape control edit elsewhere, and unedited lesion-free images. The evidence sensitivity gap (ESG) is the rate of absent-or-refer answers after erasure minus the rate after the control edit; it is zero for a model that ignores the image or reacts to both edits alike. MedScope-4B (Qwen3.5-4B, LoRA, image tools) was trained on label-derived trajectories, selected by a pre-registered rule and tested once against nine frontier and six open VLMs; four pre-registered checks then probed the mechanism. On 507 internal cases across five imaging types, MedScope-4B reached ESG 0.758 and claimed an erased lesion in 9.6% of cases, against 0.27-0.47 and"},{"tags":["Tool Use","Memory","Evaluation","Reflection"],"upvotes":0,"title":"When Should Agents Remember? Falsification-Gated Self-Evolution for LLM Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14326","id":"arxiv:2611.14326","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14326","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14326.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Tool Use","Memory","Evaluation","Reflection"],"authors":["Runxuan Tang","Haoyu Gao","Yuyan Ding","Junyi Yao","Zihao Zheng","Zecheng Sheng","Liwei Hou"],"abstract":"LLM agents increasingly operate in long-horizon, verifier-rich environments, but current self-improvement mechanisms often write episode-derived lessons directly into memory, making local success an unreliable signal for reusable and safe knowledge. This paper addresses the core question of when an experience-derived behavioral update should be admitted into persistent agent state. We propose Falsification-Gated Self-Evolution (FGSE), a verifier-grounded framework that represents each candidate lesson as a structured hypothesis with an explicit precondition, behavior change, expected effect, and verifier. FGSE applies the hypothesis only in a temporary state, tests it on target-transfer, falsification, and archived-regression probes, and then commits, refines, or rejects it according to measured gain and risk. Across web, app, tool-use, and long-term memory benchmarks, FGSE achieves stro"},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","RL for LLM","Agent Framework"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Cognitive Multi-Agent Search and Rescue: Language-Grounded Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning for Collaborative Robotics","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13161","id":"arxiv:2611.13161","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13161","submittedDate":"2026-11-09","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13161.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Reasoning","RL for LLM","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Sepideh Ravanbakhsh","Hamidreza Moradi"],"abstract":"Effective search and rescue (SAR) operations require autonomous systems capable of coordinating multiple heterogeneous agents, adapting to dynamic hazards, and aligning with human mission intent. Existing language-guided reinforcement learning (RL) methods primarily focus on single-agent instruction following and lack mechanisms for team-level coordination in complex, partially observable environments. This paper presents a Cognitive Multi-Agent Framework based on Language-Grounded Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (LG-HRL) that integrates natural-language understanding, hierarchical policy decomposition, and a shared cognitive workspace to achieve cohesive multi-agent behavior in SAR scenarios. Human instructions are interpreted by a large language model (LLM) and transformed into structured semantic cues that guide high-level strategy selection and role assignment across UAV and UGV "},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Planning","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"DrugCoding: A Scientific Programming Agent for Drug Discovery","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13651","id":"arxiv:2611.13651","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13651","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13651.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Planning","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"authors":["Junwei Hu","Zhuohao Lin","Kun Li","Hengnan Zhang"],"abstract":"Drug discovery involves multiple interconnected research stages, including molecular design, property prediction, and virtual screening. These processes are characterized by complex workflows, interdependent computational tasks, and diverse specialized tools, placing substantial demands on the coordination of scientific models and computational resources. However, existing large language model (LLM)-based scientific agents still face challenges in autonomous planning of complex tasks, adaptive selection of specialized models, and reliable execution of multi-stage scientific computations, limiting their effectiveness in automating complex drug discovery workflows. To address these challenges, we propose DrugCoding, a scientific programming agent system that integrates scientific task planning, adaptive model routing, multi-agent collaboration, and programmatic tool execution. DrugCoding e"},{"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Hallucination","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Controlled Knowledge Engineering for Taxonomy Induction with Tool-Grounded LLM Agents and Runtime-Adaptive Policies","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13966","id":"arxiv:2611.13966","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13966","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13966.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Hallucination","Evaluation"],"authors":["Jonas Limniatis","Bennet Schaarmann","Georg Reuber","Robert Stahlbock"],"abstract":"Taxonomies are structured knowledge representations that support semantic search, recommendation, product catalog organization, and retrieval-augmented generation. Their automated construction from a fixed set of candidate entities requires more than plausible text generation: the resulting hierarchy must be complete, structurally valid, and semantically coherent. Large Language Models (LLMs) are promising for this task, but iterative free-form generation remains vulnerable to omitted or hallucinated entities, invalid hierarchical relations, and inconsistent organizational principles. We introduce TIGA (Taxonomy Induction with Tool-Grounded Agents), a knowledge-engineering architecture that formulates taxonomy induction as an iterative decision process over an explicit state and constrained action space. TIGA combines executable taxonomy-editing tools, an memory that records tool calls, "},{"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"upvotes":0,"title":"LandslideFM: A Foundation Model Towards Unified Landslide Perception and Understanding","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12936","id":"arxiv:2611.12936","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12936","submittedDate":"2026-11-06","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12936.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"authors":["Shanwen Wang","Sirui Wang","Xin Sun","Jungong Han"],"abstract":"Intelligent landslide detection is essential for disaster prevention. However, existing models primarily focus on isolated visual tasks, require extensive labeled data, and lack deep analytical and scene-understanding capabilities. To address these limitations, we propose LandslideFM, the first unified foundation model that connects visual perception tasks with contextual interpretation of landslides. Specifically, LandslideFM adapts to multiple visual tasks via a Source-Agreement Graduated Trust semi-supervised learning framework that leverages limited labeled data alongside abundant unlabeled data. Furthermore, predicted landslide regions provide explicit spatial guidance to a Vision-Language Model adapted through parameter-efficient fine-tuning, enabling comprehensive landslide reasoning. To support training and evaluation in this domain, we also present LandslideVL, the first benchma"},{"tags":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Margin-Guided Token Credit for Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14015","id":"arxiv:2611.14015","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14015","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14015.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Chi Liu","Xin Chen","Yuchuan Weng","Srinivasan Manoharan"],"abstract":"In reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, a language model is rewarded for a correct final answer, and GRPO passes that reward to every token of the solution in equal measure. We instead place the credit where the model hesitated, in a method we call MCPO (Margin-Credit Policy Optimization). We measure hesitation by the top-2 margin $M=p_1-p_2$ of the policy's next-token distribution, map it to a score with two fixed thresholds, and spread each solution's advantage over its tokens so that the mean stays unchanged. The margin is read from a forward pass the trainer already performs, so the method needs no extra model evaluation and no batch statistics. We train MiniCPM5-2B-Base with LoRA for 200 steps on 2,048 DAPO-Math problems and evaluate on six competition-math benchmarks. MCPO beats an unmodified GRPO baseline for all five data seeds, by 6.55 points of macro avg@32 on averag"},{"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"upvotes":0,"title":"GTAGym: Real AAA Game Tasks Environment for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13028","id":"arxiv:2611.13028","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13028","submittedDate":"2026-11-09","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13028.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Jiajun Chen","Shicheng Fang","Jiahao Lu","Yuxin Wang","Xipeng Qiu"],"abstract":"AAA games offer a rich testbed for evaluating agent perception, reasoning, and control. However, evaluating whether agents can complete tasks in AAA games is challenging and often human-annotated because task outcomes are often difficult to verify automatically, let alone applying it for Reinforcement Learning (RL). We introduce GTAGym, an environment and framework for evaluation and RL on GTA V tasks. Its evaluation benchmark, GTAVBench, derived from GTA V main-story missions, contains 103 online tasks reconstructed across nine task families, alongside 47 image-based shooting and sniping tasks using GTA V imagery. A web server provides access to the online environment and evaluation interface, supporting parallel testing through screenshots and keyboard-mouse actions with automatic engine-state outcome checks. We evaluate six frontier LLMs on GTAVBench with textual task instructions and"},{"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"PhysMoE: Fast Spatial Grounding with Single-Token Coordinate Representation in Modality-Decoupled VLMs","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13000","id":"arxiv:2611.13000","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13000","submittedDate":"2026-11-08","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13000.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Ruimeng Liu","Denan Liang","Ruofei Bai","Fuming Zhang","Kuan Xu","Shenghai Yuan","Lihua Xie"],"abstract":"Embodied Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are backbones for robotic planning and manipulation. However, for coordinate prediction in grounding tasks, existing architectures suffer from two distinct bottlenecks: discrete tokenization disrupts spatial continuity with high decoding latency, while standard point regression fails on multi-contact affordances and irregularly shaped targets. To resolve these challenges, we propose PhysMoE, introducing three core contributions: (1) sharing lower layers for multimodal context while routing upper layers to specialized spatial (pixel) and linguistic (text) experts by physical modality, eliminating gradient interference; (2) encoding each coordinate as a single token by reusing the visual encoder's learned positional embeddings, injecting 2D spatial priors to natively align coordinates with visual features and ease training; and (3) differentiable Sign"},{"tags":["Hallucination","Alignment","Fine-tuning","Data Engine"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Actionable Feedback: Improving Model Capabilities from User Edit Data on Clinical Documentation","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12904","id":"arxiv:2611.12904","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12904","submittedDate":"2026-10-14","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12904.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Hallucination","Alignment","Fine-tuning","Data Engine"],"authors":["Daniel P. Jeong","Yewon Byun","John Giorgi","Peng Xu","Zachary C. Lipton","Michael Oberst"],"abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being deployed in clinical documentation workflows, where clinicians review and edit their outputs before use. In principle, these clinician edits represent a scalable source of supervision for model training. In practice, not all edits represent actionable feedback: for instance, on data drawn from real-world deployment of an AI clinical documentation product, our review suggests that over 45% of edited outputs contain user-added information that is not directly supported by the inputs to the model. Naively fine-tuning on such edits risks teaching the model to hallucinate. In this paper, we develop a method to address this challenge. Given that real clinician edits on deployed documentation tools are not publicly available, we first develop a taxonomy of clinician-editing behaviors, informed by data from a deployed AI clinical documentation "},{"tags":["Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation","Reflection"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Rethinking in Spikes: Mitigating Hallucinations in MDLMs with Step-Aware Decoding","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12901","id":"arxiv:2611.12901","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12901","submittedDate":"2026-10-14","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12901.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation","Reflection"],"authors":["Zhongxing Xu","Zhonghua Wang","Zhe Qian","Shiyan Su","Ming Hu","Xiaocheng Zou","Wei Feng","Mingquan Lin","Yuyin Zhou","Yifan Peng","Hamid Rezatofighi","Zongyuan Ge"],"abstract":"Recent advancements in multimodal diffusion language models (MDLMs) have exhibited strong global modeling capabilities through iterative refinement. We observe that mask uncertainty follows an overall downward trend during iterative denoising, while a few denoising steps exhibit localized entropy spikes closely associated with hallucinations. We argue that anomalous steps can be identified by measuring the entropy deviation of each denoising step from its neighboring temporal window. We hypothesize that discrete token commitment discards the distributional information at masked positions, limiting the continued interaction between candidate semantics and the context during entropy spikes. With this goal, we present StepRefine, a plug-and-play decoding strategy that leverages semantic context to achieve reliable denoising. The core of our method lies in a step-aware switch between discret"},{"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Chain of Physics: Supporting Fault Diagnosis with Engineering-Inspired Large Language Model Reasoning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14263","id":"arxiv:2611.14263","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14263","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14263.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Wenqi Zhou","Leonardo Baldo","Kai Goebel","Manuel Arias Chao"],"abstract":"Fault diagnosis in complex systems requires linking observed changes in physical quantities to the failure mechanisms that produce them. Experienced engineers perform this task by combining sensor information with knowledge of system behavior and fault propagation. Large Language Models (LLMs) can support this process, but their diagnostic reasoning depends on the availability of asset-specific engineering knowledge and on how this knowledge is represented and used during inference. This work introduces Chain of Physics (CoP), an LLM-based fault-diagnosis framework built around a Constrained Knowledge Graph (CKG) generated automatically from asset-specific reference documentation. CoP addresses failure-sensor association from textual engineering knowledge. The CKG stores diagnostic chains connecting failure modes, changes in physical quantities, and monitoring sensors. During inference, "},{"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection","RL for LLM"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Curriculum proof repair with learned counterexamples","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13627","id":"arxiv:2611.13627","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13627","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13627.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection","RL for LLM"],"authors":["Yuechun Sun","Jun Yang","Kexin Pei"],"abstract":"Language models (LMs) have substantially lowered the barrier to applying formal verification for building reliable and secure software. Existing approaches commonly follow the verifier-in-the-loop paradigm, but the verifier often provides only coarse, binary pass/fail feedback, which offers little actionable signal on how the proof should be repaired. Recent work shows that counterexamples provide more actionable feedback by grounding proof failures in concrete program states, but generating useful ones at inference time requires costly iterative refinement. We present TopoVerus, an LM-based framework for Verus proof repair that internalizes counterexample-guided diagnosis as a model capability, producing useful counterexamples in a single pass. Our key insight is to train counterexample generation not as a standalone validation task, but as a data-driven diagnostic objective: a countere"},{"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Interpretability"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Understanding Mixture-of-Experts Language Models Beyond Expert Boundaries","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13975","id":"arxiv:2611.13975","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13975","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13975.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Interpretability"],"authors":["Zhenhong Zhou","Mao Zheng","Mingyang Song","Qiankun Li","Kun Wang","Junfeng Fang"],"abstract":"In Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models, the nature of expert specialization remains controversial. Reports of domain-associated expert preferences contrast with analyses attributing routing primarily to token identity or syntactic patterns. To clarify these differing accounts, we look beyond expert-level routing to investigate the neuron-level organization of domain-specialized computation using Routing-Aware Magnitude Profiling (RAMP), a measure developed for this analysis. Using mixed-domain calibration data, we identify domain-specialized neuron sets with 58-74% overlap across multi-source samples of the same domain, compared with 15-24% across domains. To understand how this organization evolves during training, we trace neuron importance throughout pre-training and post-training across three fully open MoE families. Neuron importance continues to reorganize between pre-training checkpoi"},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Toward Autonomous RTL and Synthesis Optimization: Benchmarking and Self-Improving Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13713","id":"arxiv:2611.13713","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13713","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13713.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Wenji Fang","Yao Lu","Jinghua Wang","Zhiyao Xie"],"abstract":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and autonomous agents are creating new opportunities for hardware design optimization. This paper examines three connected directions toward autonomous RTL and synthesis optimization: (1) Realistic benchmarking. We discuss reference-based and open-ended RTL optimization benchmarks that evaluate optimization opportunities under realistic EDA workflows. (2) Tool-grounded RTL optimization. We present Dr. RTL, a multi-agent framework that automates critical-path analysis, RTL rewriting, and implementation-level evaluation, while continuously improving through reusable optimization skills. Evaluated on 20 real-world RTL designs, Dr.~RTL achieves average WNS/TNS improvements of 21\\%/17\\% with a 6\\% area reduction over commercial synthesis. (3) Design-specific synthesis optimization. We explore agent-driven synthesis script tuning and the discover"},{"tags":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Agent Architecture Cost-Success Trade-offs: A Controlled Evaluation across Enterprise-Style SOPs","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12953","id":"arxiv:2611.12953","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12953","submittedDate":"2026-11-07","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12953.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation","Agent Framework"],"authors":["Subhrangshu Nandi","Hitesh Arora","Yunfei Bai","Udita Patel","Chirag Shah","Hannah Kwon","Wei Zhang","Weian Chen","Chloe Ruoqi Li","Alberto Sconfienza","Ayush Goyal","Yasmine Lee","Ming Xiong","Prathap Ramachandra"],"abstract":"Enterprises increasingly use large language model agents to execute standard operating procedures (SOPs): long, branching, tool-intensive workflows. Yet there is little controlled evidence for choosing among competing agentic architectures. Holding the model, tasks, and tools fixed and varying only the architecture, we compare four designs (a function-calling single agent, a skill-augmented agent, centralized multi-agent orchestration, and a triage swarm; eight arms) across 13 enterprise SOPs, and introduce a skill-definition quality rubric to build matched high and low-quality skill corpora. Three takeaways emerge. (1)~\\textbf{There is no universal best architecture; the right choice is SOP-dependent.} On a given SOP, task success ranges from under $10\\%$ on the hardest to near $100\\%$ on the easiest; no architecture is statistically separable in aggregate, and added structure only plau"},{"tags":["Reasoning","Reflection","Multimodal Agent"],"upvotes":0,"title":"A Cross-Scenario Survey and Analysis of Self-Evolving Multimodal Agents","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14256","id":"arxiv:2611.14256","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14256","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14256.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Reflection","Multimodal Agent"],"authors":["Jingjing Jiang"],"abstract":"Recent work shows that multimodal agents increasingly self-improve without recurring human annotation. However, each self-improvement mechanism is designed and evaluated within a single scenario, leaving its preconditions unverified under cross-scenario transfer. This survey investigates the structural source of asymmetric mechanism transfer across four scenarios: multimodal reasoning, multimodal generation, GUI and Web navigation, and embodied manipulation. We first formalize the self-evolving multimodal agent by four criteria and a differentia of self-reference, and adopt the three-stage self-evolving loop of experience generation, feedback acquisition, and update consolidation as our analytical framework to position the scenarios along ceilings on rollout count and feedback fidelity. Comparing self-evolving with fixed loops within each scenario separates the limitations that the scena"},{"tags":["Reasoning","Hallucination","Reflection"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Cognitive Mapping of Learning by Thinking Mechanisms in Large Language Models","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14382","id":"arxiv:2611.14382","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14382","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14382.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Hallucination","Reflection"],"authors":["Dong Han","Jin Zhang","Kunyue Yang"],"abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly exhibit capabilities that resemble human Learning by Thinking (LbT), the acquisition of new insight through internal processes such as explanation and reasoning, without new external input. A systematic account of the cognitive mechanisms behind these capabilities, and of their limits, remains underdeveloped. This paper proposes a Cognitive LbT Framework that maps four mechanisms (Reflective Reasoning, Synthetic Memory Formation, Internal Simulation, and Conceptual Self-Organization) onto computational processes in LLMs. Drawing on representative methods, including chain-of-thought prompting, self-consistency, self-reflection, Tree-of-Thoughts search, Self-Instruct, and self-taught reasoning, the analysis argues that selected methods can functionally approximate aspects of these mechanisms and can improve performance on some tasks without additio"},{"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Graph Visualizations in Prompts for Knowledge Graph Question Answering","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12938","id":"arxiv:2611.12938","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12938","submittedDate":"2026-11-06","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12938.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Luiz do Valle Miranda","Andreas L. Opdahl","Grzegorz J. Nalepa"],"abstract":"We study when and how multimodal input improves knowledge graph question answering (KGQA) in large vision--language models. Using a controlled evaluation over 1,500 WebQSP and CWQ questions and six VLMs within a fixed KGQA pipeline, we compare text-only prompting against text-plus-graph visualization at the per-question level and analyze performance changes as a function of reasoning complexity, model size, and image layout. Adding a graph image yields an average gain of 1.85 F1 points, driven by strong precision improvements on low-complexity questions (+4.65 F1). Mixed-effects and regression analyses show that these multimodal gains do not depend strongly on model scale, but are highly sensitive to graph layout: image-only settings sharply degrade performance, hierarchical Graphviz layouts outperform less organized renderings, and decomposing large subgraphs into separated paths yields"},{"tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"upvotes":0,"title":"F3-AGI v4.0: Bioenergetically-Gated Active Inference, Metaplastic Preconditioning, and Residual Program Synthesis for Sample-Efficient Abstract Reasoning","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13325","id":"arxiv:2611.13325","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13325","submittedDate":"2026-11-09","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13325.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"authors":["Henrique Oliveira"],"abstract":"Few-shot abstract reasoning on ARC-AGI requires inducing algorithmic transformations from 2 to 5 demonstration pairs without task-specific pretraining. Because few-shot demonstrations under-constrain the hypothesis space, program synthesis suffers from the Rashomon effect (predictive multiplicity): heuristic search finds spurious programs with low training error that fail on test inputs, while unconstrained library growth degrades latency. We introduce F3-AGI v4.0, a CPU-native neuro-symbolic and active inference architecture comprising three mechanisms: (1) F3-Metabolic-Optim, combining mistake-gated plasticity, Fisher-EMA preconditioning, and virtual ATP gating; (2) Adaptive Complexity-Guided Program Synthesis (AACR) with a Cycle-3 ResidualPatchSynthesizer and smallest-pair early rejection; and (3) a 512D L2-normalized random projection driving Expected Free Energy (EFE) exploration wi"},{"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning"],"upvotes":0,"title":"A computational framework for understanding embodied plans","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13669","id":"arxiv:2611.13669","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13669","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13669.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning"],"authors":["Aalap D. Shah","Basil Saeed","Joshua B. Tenenbaum","Tobias Gerstenberg","Hyowon Gweon","Ilker Yildirim"],"abstract":"Reasoning about people's plans and goals based on their actions is fundamental to human social interaction and essential for cooperative, embodied artificial intelligence. Formalizing this capacity in naturalistic, embodied settings has been a longstanding challenge. Here, we present GenEA, a novel agent representation capable of reasoning over multi-step, long-horizon embodied plans. At the heart of GenEA lies a multilevel state-space that couples the coarse biomechanics of an articulated body and its interactions with nearby objects by extending the task-and-motion planning framework from robotics with simulation-based intuitive physics. By embedding this state-space in a generative model, GenEA yields a joint probability distribution over goals and embodied plans. Across several experiments spanning the qualitatively distinct domains of object manipulation and navigation, featuring di"},{"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Interpretability"],"upvotes":0,"title":"Breakdown of Autoregressive Multi-Step Reasoning Under Concentrated vs. Diffuse Attention Head Dropout","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14209","id":"arxiv:2611.14209","date":"2026-11-11","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14209","submittedDate":"2026-11-10","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14209.pdf","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-11-11","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Interpretability"],"authors":["Karol Struniawski"],"abstract":"During extended wakefulness, mammalian neocortical networks experience localized periods of transient neuronal silence termed off-states. Here, we examine how analogous spatiotemporally concentrated disruptions affect sequential reasoning in autoregressive Large Language Models (LLMs). Evaluating 250 paired derivations on the GSM8K benchmark across two 8B models (Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct), we contrast clustered bursts of attention head masking against budget-matched diffuse i.i.d. dropout. Concentrated bursts induce substantial reasoning degradation: at onset parameter $\\mathcal{B}=0.10$ (scheduling $\\sim 5.7\\%$ of vulnerable head-token volume), Qwen3-8B accuracy drops from $91.2\\%$ under diffuse noise to $78.8\\%$ under burst dropout ($Δ= -12.4$ percentage points [pp], exact McNemar $p = 5.5 \\times 10^{-6}$), while LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct drops from $84.0\\%$ to $76.8\\%$ ($Δ= -7.2"}],"archive":[],"latestPath":"data/latest.json"};
